audiomixer: level 1.0 passes samples through unchanged without ARM

On ARM Cortex-M4 and up, Mixer.c multiplies a sample by a voice's level
with smulwb, which is exact. Every other build (unix, the Xtensa ESP32
ports) fell back to a float divide by (1 << 15) - 1. The level is a Q15
number where 1.0 is 32768, so a voice at level 1.0 had a gain of
32768/32767, and any sample above about 32740 gained a step. A bare mixer
turned -32752 into -32753.

A level of exactly 1.0 now copies the sample. Every other level keeps
the float multiply it had, so a quieter voice scales as before.

diff --git a/shared-module/audiomixer/Mixer.c b/shared-module/audiomixer/Mixer.c
index 464117f..c7109c7 100644
--- a/shared-module/audiomixer/Mixer.c
+++ b/shared-module/audiomixer/Mixer.c
@@ -108,8 +108,14 @@ static inline uint32_t mult16signed(uint32_t val, int32_t lomul, int32_t himul)
     #else
     uint32_t result = 0;
     for (int8_t i = 0; i < 2; i++) {
-        float mod_mul = (float)(i ? himul : lomul) / (float)((1 << 15) - 1);
+        int32_t mul = i ? himul : lomul;
         int16_t ai = (val >> (sizeof(uint16_t) * 8 * i));
+        if (mul == (1 << 15)) {
+            // Level 1.0 passes the sample through unchanged.
+            result |= (((uint32_t)ai) & 0xffff) << (sizeof(int16_t) * 8 * i);
+            continue;
+        }
+        float mod_mul = (float)mul / (float)((1 << 15) - 1);
         int32_t intermediate = (int32_t)(ai * mod_mul);
         if (intermediate > SHRT_MAX) {
             intermediate = SHRT_MAX;
